The column name 'us_lab' invites the reading 'conducted in a lab'. That reading is wrong.
Evidence that us_lab.* is a US-location dummy, not a setting indicator:
  1. us_lab.o == (experiment_country.o == 'United States') on 926/926 rows of the full
     file, with ZERO disagreements. Within ee the 11 us_lab.o==1 studies are exactly the
     11 with experiment_country.o=='United States'; the 7 us_lab.o==0 studies are
     Australia, Austria, Germany x2, Switzerland x2, United Kingdom: {'Germany': 2, 'Switzerland': 2, 'United Kingdom': 1, 'Australia': 1, 'Austria': 1}
  2. 40 rows in the full file have online.o==1 AND us_lab.o==1, i.e. internet
     experiments are coded us_lab==1 - impossible under a lab reading.
  3. lab_id, the only genuine lab-identity field, is 0/18 populated in ee.

Mechanically us_lab.o==1 selects 11/18 = 61.1%, but that quantity estimates the
share of ee originals run in the United States, not the share run in labs.
It is reported here only to demonstrate why the naive column-name reading must be
rejected, and is excluded from the headline, from the rules table in section 3, and
from the submitted hypothesis text.

