------------------------------------------------------------------------------
CAVEATS
------------------------------------------------------------------------------
C1 DEFINITIONAL: 'lab setting' is not a recorded variable. R1 = not-online. A stricter reading
   that requires a captive student pool gives 17/18 (ee.4 'Participation' uses a community
   pool). The choice of rule, not the data, drives the 100% vs 94.4% difference.
C2 ZERO-VARIANCE / NO SAMPLING UNCERTAINTY: online.o is constant within ee, so no interval is
   meaningful; reporting a CI over n=18 would dress a degenerate distribution as an estimate.
C3 COVERAGE: lab_id, the only field that could identify an actual lab, is 0/18 populated;
   us_lab.* is a US-location dummy (926/926 agreement with country=='United States'; 40
   online studies carry us_lab==1) and was therefore excluded, not merely avoided.
C4 DENOMINATOR PROVENANCE (EXTERNAL, NOT DATA FROM THIS ENVIRONMENT): these files contain 18
   ee rows and that is the denominator used. Published accounts of the EERP describe a
   candidate set larger than the set actually replicated; if 'studies in Experimental
   Economics' were defined over such a candidate list rather than over replicated pairs, the
   denominator and hence the proportion could change. This is flagged as an external caveat;
   no external count is imported into the estimate, and no per-study external verification of
   lab location was performed - nothing outside these two CSVs was consulted.
C5 SCOPE: the answer is a hypothesis about these files, not a measured fact about the studies.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------
SUBMITTED HYPOTHESIS
------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Hypothesis: essentially all Experimental Economics studies in this dataset were conducted in a lab (in-person) setting. Of the 18 original studies in the ee (Experimental Economics) replication project, 18 of 18 (100%) are coded online.o == 0, i.e. their original data were not collected online; all 18 are also in-person on the replication side (online.r == 0) and all 18 paid subjects in cash. Under a stricter definition that additionally requires a student subject pool the figure is 17 of 18 (94.4%), the sole exception being ee.4 ('Participation'), which recruited a community rather than a student pool; so the proportion is 1.00 under the primary definition and no lower than about 0.94 under the stricter one. Two things qualify that number. First, it rests on a modelling assumption rather than on documentation: neither file records a 'lab setting' variable, so 'lab' is operationalised as the negation of online data collection, and the only field that could identify a specific lab (lab_id) is empty for all 18 rows. Second, because online.o has zero variance within ee, this restates a degenerate distribution rather than estimating a sample proportion - there is no meaningful sampling uncertainty to attach, and the residual uncertainty is definitional and coverage-related. Within these files the ee corpus is distinctly in-person (0 of 18 originals online, against 37 of 592 in Many Labs 1), so the substantive claim is that Experimental Economics in this replication corpus is an in-person, laboratory-based enterprise - while noting that 'not online' cannot by itself separate a physical lab from some other in-person venue, and that the estimate covers the 18 replicated pairs present in these files rather than any wider candidate list.
